#!/bin/bash
# land-letter-archive.sh — every letter carries its date, and both letter pages
# keep the letters before them.
#
# PRECONDITIONS, in this order:
#   land-letter-page.sh, land-observatory-paper.sh   (this tree's CSS)
#   land-monthly-history.sh                          (the endpoint the month reads)
#
# FRONTEND ONLY (together-city-react). Push, and Vercel ships it.
set -euo pipefail

cd "$(dirname "$0")"
[ -d together-city-react ] || { echo "!! Run this from the Together-Ai repo root."; exit 1; }

if [ -f .git/index.lock ] && [ ! -s .git/index.lock ]; then
  echo "== clearing a stale empty .git/index.lock"
  rm -f .git/index.lock
fi

NEEDS="A letter keeps its date"
LOG="$(git log --oneline -40)"
case "$LOG" in
  *"$NEEDS"*) echo "== \"$NEEDS\" is already here. Nothing to do."; exit 0 ;;
esac
case "$LOG" in
  *"The observatory is the rail now"*) ;;
  *) echo "!! Run land-observatory-paper.sh first — this is written against the tree it produces."; exit 1 ;;
esac
case "$LOG" in
  *"A month is kept as long as a month is remembered"*) ;;
  *) echo "!! Run land-monthly-history.sh first — the month's archive reads an endpoint it adds."; exit 1 ;;
esac

ALLOWED='^ M together-city-react/(public/assets/img/(apple-touch-icon-180|tc-icon-1024|tc-icon-192|tc-icon-512|tc-icon-maskable-512)\.png|public/downloads/TogetherCity\.apk)$'
DIRTY="$(git status --porcelain \
  | grep -Ev '^\?\? (land-.*\.sh|push-.*\.sh|.*\.patch)$' \
  | grep -Ev "$ALLOWED" || true)"
if [ -n "$DIRTY" ]; then
  echo "!! The tree carries changes this script did not expect:"; echo "$DIRTY"; exit 1
fi

PATCH="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/land.XXXXXX")"
trap 'rm -f "$PATCH"' EXIT
cat <<'B64EOF' | tr -d '\n' | openssl base64 -d -A > "$PATCH"

B64EOF
WANT="7402e94322caf502abee73d6d9879b8ad2bce2e8a395a4153d8257133a40faac"
GOT="$(shasum -a 256 "$PATCH" | awk '{print $1}')"
[ "$GOT" = "$WANT" ] || { echo "!! Patch is corrupt."; echo "   want $WANT"; echo "   got  $GOT"; exit 1; }
echo "== patch verified"
git apply --check -C1 "$PATCH" || { echo "!! Patch does not apply cleanly. Nothing written."; exit 1; }
git apply -C1 "$PATCH"
echo "== applied"

cd together-city-react
npx tsc --noEmit
npx vitest run
node scripts/a11y-audit.mjs
node scripts/motion-ceiling.mjs
node scripts/lint-ceiling.mjs
npx vite build
cd ..

git add together-city-react/src/styles/tokens.css \
        together-city-react/src/styles/layout.css \
        together-city-react/src/app/relief.spec.ts \
        together-city-react/src/features/astrology/api.ts \
        together-city-react/src/features/astrology/hooks.ts \
        together-city-react/src/features/astrology/components/Letter.tsx \
        together-city-react/src/features/astrology/pages/AstroToday.tsx \
        together-city-react/src/features/astrology/pages/AstroMonthly.tsx
git commit -F - <<'MSG'
A letter keeps its date

The owner asked for two things in one sentence — put today's date on the letter,
and keep the letters by date so they can be read back — and then, seeing it,
for the month to do the same. They are one thing. A letter without a date is a
page; a letter with one is a record, and a record is worth keeping.

THE DATE IS THE LABEL, NOT A NEW LINE. The small uppercase line above the title
already said "TODAY". It says "TODAY · TUESDAY, 11 AUGUST 2026" now, and on an
archived letter it drops the word and gives only the day. That is one label
doing what a label is for. The alternative was a dateline under the title,
which is a second small grey line on a page whose entire argument is that it
has no furniture. The month already had its date as the label — "AUGUST 2026" —
which is why it needed nothing here and is the reason the daily now matches it
rather than the other way round.

THE ARCHIVE IS THE SAME PAGE, NOT ANOTHER ONE.

What was there before, on the daily only, was a card-shaped toggle reading
"Earlier letters (7)" that unrolled all seven letters underneath today's. That
is a scroll with nothing to aim at: nobody remembers a scroll position, they
remember a Tuesday. The foot of each page is now a list of DATES — the day or
the month, and the title that letter was given — and choosing one prints that
letter in the composition above. Same paper, same measure, same type, same
title treatment. A letter you kept is not displayed differently from the letter
that arrived this morning; it is the same object, on a different date.

The way back out is a line at the end of the letter you just read, not a
control in a bar above it. You finish reading the 8th, and the next thing you
want is today's.

THE MONTH MATTERS MORE HERE THAN THE DAY, AND IT ARRIVED SECOND. A daily letter
is read on the day it comes. A monthly one is the kind somebody goes back to in
November to see what August said — which is why the server keeps two years of
those against the daily's thirty days, and why the page that had no archive at
all was the one that needed it most.

ONE COMPONENT, TWO PAGES, AND THE PAGES HAND IT ROWS RATHER THAN LETTERS.
`LetterArchive` lays out; it does not know what a letter is. The daily writes
out "Monday, 10 August" from an ISO day, and the monthly prints the `month`
string the server stored when the letter was written. That split is not
tidiness: formatting a month from a key in the browser's timezone is how you
show somebody a letter labelled with the wrong month, because `new Date('2026-08')`
is UTC midnight and therefore July for everybody west of Greenwich. The one
place that fallback is still needed — a letter old enough to predate the field —
builds the date from parts instead of parsing the string.

WHY NOT A ROUTE PER DATE. `/astrology/today/2026-08-09` would be honest, and it
would also put a date into the breadcrumb, the browser history and the recent
trail — three separate places that would then have to learn what an archived
letter is. The sidebar says "Today · Your letter for today" and it stays true:
you are on the Today page, reading back. The moment somebody needs to send one
day to somebody else, that is the moment for a route, and it is a small change
from here.

A LETTER WITH NO TITLE STILL LISTS. Titles arrived with the v6 letters;
everything written before has none. The row prints the date and stops rather
than inventing one, which is why `title` is optional in the api type and why
the archive reads correctly on the day this ships, when most rows are dates
alone.

THE TITLE IN THE LIST WEARS THE LETTER'S OWN SERIF, and that needed the design
system's permission. relief.spec.ts assertion 4 allows exactly one selector
outside the nutrition press to read `--press-serif`, asserted by name. Rather
than add a second grant, the rule became ONE selector with two places in it —
`.letter-title, .letter-archive-day .t` — so the title on the letter and the
same title in the list cannot drift into different faces. The assertion moved
with it and still fails on a third borrower.

AND `.letter-link` FINALLY HAS A DEFINITION. It was styled only as
`.letter-past .letter-link`, and when the letter became a printed page the
`.letter-past` wrapper went with the card — which left "Try again", "Check
again" and "Add your details" rendering as raw browser buttons on the paper, on
both letter pages. They are underlined text now, in the letter's ink, with the
underline as the focus affordance. That was a live defect on the current build
and it is fixed here because it is the same block of rules.

The one line on either page is the rule above the archive, and it is a new
token: `--letter-rule`, the letter's own ink at 14% rather than a picked grey,
so it sits on whatever the paper becomes. A divider earns itself exactly once —
between the letter and a list that genuinely is a different kind of thing. The
archive renders nothing at all when there is nothing behind you; a heading over
an empty rule on somebody's first day is the interface pointing at an absence.

Verified by rendering rather than by reading, at 1440 and at 390, on both
pages: date in sans and title in Instrument Serif on one baseline on the
desktop, stacked on the phone, a title-less row degrading to the date alone.

tsc clean, vitest green (54 files, 437 tests), a11y / motion / lint ceilings
clear, vite build clean.

MSG
echo "== committed"

echo
echo "==============================================================="
echo " Landed: $NEEDS"
echo " Both letters are dated, and the foot of each page is every"
echo " letter before it. Push, and Vercel ships it."
echo "==============================================================="
